.TH SSH-OTP 1 "September 2026" "ssh-otp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ssh-otp \- issue a single-use SSH passphrase for an existing account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh-otp
.I username duration
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints four independently random EFF wordlist words separated by equals signs.
The credential permits one SSH authentication, not one command or session.
It is consumed before authentication success is returned and cannot be replayed.
A failed account check or disconnected client does not restore it.
When no active credential exists, authentication is rejected without a password
prompt or a burner-specific explanation. SSH reports its generic authentication
failure. Public-key authentication remains available.
.PP
Duration is a positive integer followed by s, m, or h; minimum 1s, maximum 1h.
For example: ssh-otp hayk 10m.
The issuer waits until use, expiry, replacement, or interruption.
Ctrl-C, SIGHUP and SIGTERM revoke an outstanding credential.
SIGKILL or a crash may leave it usable until its original deadline; expiry is
independently enforced by the PAM module using the boot clock, including suspend.
Expired bookkeeping is removed on the next access or reboot.
.PP
Five incorrect candidate passphrases invalidate the outstanding credential.
Issuing a new credential replaces the previous one. Existing sessions continue
after consumption, revocation or expiry. The credential is not a sudo password.
.PP
Non-root callers may issue only for their real UID. Root may issue for any existing
account. No accounts are created and no account, SSH authorization or sudo
restrictions are removed. Secret output requires a terminal.
.SH CLIENT
Use the existing Windows OpenSSH client: ssh username@server.
If a client disables interactive authentication, use:
.PP
ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard-interactive username@server
.PP
Verify the server host-key fingerprint through a trusted channel.
.SH SECURITY
This avoids placing a reusable SSH key on a borrowed computer. It does not
protect against malware controlling the client, stealing the phrase before use,
controlling the session, or capturing a reusable sudo password.
Do not enable SSH agent forwarding to an untrusted computer.
.PP
The CLI is installed root-owned with mode 4755. The PAM module and credential
store must remain root-controlled. Store files contain salted SHA-256 verifiers,
not plaintext. Four words from 7776 entries provide approximately 51.7 bits.
An attacker with root access can subvert authentication regardless of this tool.
.SH FILES
.TP
/run/ssh-otp
Root-only, locked runtime credential store; cleared at reboot.
.TP
/etc/pam.d/sshd
Installer replaces audited password-authentication rules using the selected
distribution profile. Other PAM service files are untouched; recognized
authorization gates and account/session policies remain in place.
.TP
/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-ssh-otp.conf
Enables PAM keyboard-interactive and preserves public-key access. Ordinary SSH
password authentication remains disabled.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for consumed, invalidated, expired or replaced credentials; 1 on error;
128 plus signal number for a caught interruption.
.SH SOURCE
https://github.com/IfkumRfnl/ssh-otp
